  "British-French; the comic verse and the polemical essay",
  "Poet, essayist, historian and controversialist; the author of Cautionary Tales and one of the great English comic poets.",
  "Born Joseph Hilaire Pierre Rene Belloc at La Celle-Saint-Cloud near Paris on 27 July 1870, the son of a French barrister and an English mother; he was educated at the Oratory and Balliol, and became a naturalised Briton in 1902. The Bad Child's Book of Beasts (1896), The Path to Rome (1902), Cautionary Tales for Children (1907) and his histories and polemics followed. He died at Guildford on 16 July 1953.",
  "Belloc wrote the funniest children's verse in English and one of the great English travel books: Cautionary Tales made the comic moral poem a permanent form, The Path to Rome invented the modern walking book, and his polemics made him the most pugnacious Catholic man of letters of the century.",
